{
  "index": 21,
  "response": "Imagine you're teaching a child how to fold a fitted sheet. \n\nYou say, \"First, take one corner and tuck it into another corner.\"  That seems easy enough! But then they look at the remaining corners and ask, \"How do I do the rest?\"\n\nSo, you say, \"It's just like what we did before! Find a corner and tuck it into another corner.\" \n\nThey repeat this process, each time folding a new set of corners, just like how they folded the first set.\n\nThis is recursion in action! It's a problem-solving method where you solve a smaller version of the same problem repeatedly until you reach a simple base case that you already know how to solve. Then, you combine those solutions to solve the original problem.\n\n\nThink of it like a set of Russian nesting dolls. Each doll is a smaller version of the larger one. You open each doll to reveal another smaller doll inside until you get to the smallest one.  \n\nIn recursion, we keep breaking down a problem into smaller, similar problems until we reach that simplest \"smallest doll\" \u2013 the base case. Then, we work our way back up, combining the solutions of those smaller problems to solve the original, larger problem.\n\n\nDoes that make sense?",
